## Installing

Access a Kubernetes cluster.

If you have [kind](https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/) installed, create a local cluster and install the helm chart using the following commands:

```bash
make helm-lint
make kind-load
make helm-install
```

Or directly with Helm:

```bash
helm upgrade --install {{ template "chart.name" . }} -f helm/{{ template "chart.name" . }}/values.yaml helm/{{ template "chart.name" . }} --namespace {{ template "chart.name" . }} --create-namespace
```

Create a port-forward with the follow command:

```bash
kubectl port-forward svc/{{ template "chart.name" . }} 8080:8080 -n {{ template "chart.name" . }}
```

Open the browser and access the URL: http://localhost:8080

## Health checks, logs and tracing

- **Probes**: the web Deployment and (with `mcp.enabled`) the MCP Deployment both use `httpGet` probes — `livenessProbe.path` (`/healthz`) and `readinessProbe.path` (`/readyz`) on their own container port. `/readyz` returns `503` from the moment the pod receives SIGTERM; the app then keeps serving for `env.shutdownDelay` (default `5s`) so the pod leaves the Service endpoints, and drains in-flight requests for up to 20s — keep `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above `shutdownDelay + 20s`.
- **helm test**: `make helm-test` (or `helm test <release>`) calls `/healthz` and `/readyz` through the web Service, and through the MCP Service when `mcp.enabled=true`.
- **Logs**: JSON, one object per line, on stderr — ready for any cluster log agent (Grafana Alloy, Fluent Bit, Vector, ...) without a parser config beyond "JSON".
- **Tracing**: `--set tracing.enabled=true --set tracing.endpoint=http://<otlp-collector>:4318` exports OpenTelemetry traces (OTLP/HTTP) from both Deployments, with `service.name` `mytoolkit` / `mytoolkit-mcp`; `tracing.sampler`/`samplerArg`/`resourceAttributes` map to the standard `OTEL_*` variables. A ConfigMap change rolls the pods (`checksum/config` annotation).
- **Metrics**: Prometheus scrape annotations on both pods, plus optional `ServiceMonitor`s (`metrics.serviceMonitor.enabled`).

## Uninstalling

```bash
helm uninstall {{ template "chart.name" . }} --namespace {{ template "chart.name" . }}
```

Or with the Makefile:

```bash
make helm-uninstall
```

